WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.148
<v Examiner>Three marks.

2
00:00:02.148 --> 00:00:06.515
<v Examiner>Define complementary and alternative medicine, with an example of each,

3
00:00:06.515 --> 00:00:11.744
<v Examiner>and describe the nurse's response when a patient plans to stop their treatment for an

4
00:00:11.744 --> 00:00:12.482
<v Examiner>alternative.

5
00:00:12.932 --> 00:00:18.142
Complementary means used alongside conventional medicine, in addition to it.

6
00:00:18.142 --> 00:00:22.049
Her formative example: an osteopath alongside medication.

7
00:00:22.049 --> 00:00:25.887
Alternative means used instead of conventional medicine,

8
00:00:25.887 --> 00:00:29.657
like herbs and diet instead of chemotherapy for cancer.

9
00:00:30.107 --> 00:00:33.087
The response, her likely best-response question.

10
00:00:33.087 --> 00:00:34.266
Don't dismiss them.

11
00:00:34.266 --> 00:00:39.355
Talk with them and explore their reasons, because online misinformation is common.

12
00:00:39.355 --> 00:00:42.707
Give accurate information so their choice is informed,

13
00:00:42.707 --> 00:00:46.431
involve the healthcare team and the doctor, and document it.

14
00:00:46.431 --> 00:00:47.921
It's still their choice.

15
00:00:48.371 --> 00:00:53.837
Rongoā Māori is Aotearoa's own traditional healing, with a strong spiritual element.

16
00:00:53.837 --> 00:00:58.588
It has three parts: mirimiri, which is massage; karakia, which is prayer;

17
00:00:58.588 --> 00:01:00.996
and rongoā rākau, the plant remedies.

18
00:01:00.996 --> 00:01:05.681
The tohunga or rongoā practitioner is responsible for it, not the nurse.

19
00:01:05.681 --> 00:01:09.261
If a patient wants to use it alongside their medicines,

20
00:01:09.261 --> 00:01:14.662
the nurse supports a discussion with everyone involved and checks for interactions.

21
00:01:15.112 --> 00:01:20.699
Complementary: alongside conventional medicine, like an osteopath with medication.

22
00:01:20.699 --> 00:01:22.539
Alternative: instead of it.

23
00:01:22.539 --> 00:01:28.262
If someone plans to stop treatment, explore why, inform, involve the team, document,

24
00:01:28.262 --> 00:01:29.557
respect the choice.

25
00:01:29.557 --> 00:01:35.144
Rongoā Māori: mirimiri, karakia and rongoā rākau; the practitioner is responsible.
